NNAAMMEE
       chpasswd - update passwords in batch mode

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       cchhppaasssswwdd [_o_p_t_i_o_n_s]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The cchhppaasssswwdd command reads a list of user name and password pairs from
       standard input and uses this information to update a group of existing
       users. Each line is of the format:

       _u_s_e_r___n_a_m_e:_p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d

       By default the passwords must be supplied in clear-text, and are
       encrypted by cchhppaasssswwdd. Also the password age will be updated, if
       present.

       By default, passwords are encrypted by PAM, but (even if not
       recommended) you can select a different encryption method with the --ee,
       --mm, or --cc options.

       Except when PAM is used to encrypt the passwords, cchhppaasssswwdd first
       updates all the passwords in memory, and then commits all the changes
       to disk if no errors occurred for any user.

       When PAM is used to encrypt the passwords (and update the passwords in
       the system database) then if a password cannot be updated cchhppaasssswwdd
       continues updating the passwords of the next users, and will return an
       error code on exit.

       This command is intended to be used in a large system environment where
       many accounts are created at a single time.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       The options which apply to the cchhppaasssswwdd command are:

       --cc, ----ccrryypptt--mmeetthhoodd _M_E_T_H_O_D
           Use the specified method to encrypt the passwords.

           The available methods are DES, MD5, NONE, and SHA256 or SHA512 if
           your libc support these methods.

           By default, PAM is used to encrypt the passwords.

       --ee, ----eennccrryypptteedd
           Supplied passwords are in encrypted form.

       --hh, ----hheellpp
           Display help message and exit.

       --mm, ----mmdd55
           Use MD5 encryption instead of DES when the supplied passwords are
           not encrypted.

       --RR, ----rroooott _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R
           Apply changes in the _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R directory and use the configuration
           files from the _C_H_R_O_O_T___D_I_R directory. Only absolute paths are
           supported.

       --ss, ----sshhaa--rroouunnddss _R_O_U_N_D_S
           Use the specified number of rounds to encrypt the passwords.

           The value 0 means that the system will choose the default number of
           rounds for the crypt method (5000).

           A minimal value of 1000 and a maximal value of 999,999,999 will be
           enforced.

           You can only use this option with the SHA256 or SHA512 crypt
           method.

           By default, the number of rounds is defined by the
           SSHHAA__CCRRYYPPTT__MMIINN__RROOUUNNDDSS and SSHHAA__CCRRYYPPTT__MMAAXX__RROOUUNNDDSS variables in
           /etc/login.defs.

CCAAVVEEAATTSS
       Remember to set permissions or umask to prevent readability of
       unencrypted files by other users.

CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN
       The following configuration variables in /etc/login.defs change the
       behavior of this tool:

       SSHHAA__CCRRYYPPTT__MMIINN__RROOUUNNDDSS (number), SSHHAA__CCRRYYPPTT__MMAAXX__RROOUUNNDDSS (number)
           When EENNCCRRYYPPTT__MMEETTHHOODD is set to _S_H_A_2_5_6 or _S_H_A_5_1_2, this defines the
           number of SHA rounds used by the encryption algorithm by default
           (when the number of rounds is not specified on the command line).

           With a lot of rounds, it is more difficult to brute forcing the
           password. But note also that more CPU resources will be needed to
           authenticate users.

           If not specified, the libc will choose the default number of rounds
           (5000), which is orders of magnitude too low for modern hardware.

           The values must be inside the 1000-999,999,999 range.

           If only one of the SSHHAA__CCRRYYPPTT__MMIINN__RROOUUNNDDSS or SSHHAA__CCRRYYPPTT__MMAAXX__RROOUUNNDDSS
           values is set, then this value will be used.

           If SSHHAA__CCRRYYPPTT__MMIINN__RROOUUNNDDSS > SSHHAA__CCRRYYPPTT__MMAAXX__RROOUUNNDDSS, the highest value
           will be used.

           Note: This only affect the generation of group passwords. The
           generation of user passwords is done by PAM and subject to the PAM
           configuration. It is recommended to set this variable consistently
           with the PAM configuration.

FFIILLEESS
       /etc/passwd
           User account information.

       /etc/shadow
           Secure user account information.

       /etc/login.defs
           Shadow password suite configuration.

       /etc/pam.d/chpasswd
           PAM configuration for cchhppaasssswwdd.

